1. From time-to-time, the CBDT directs by instruction to all Officers of the Department that where the aggregate value of international transaction exceeds a certain sum, it must refer the matter of determination of ALP to the Transfer Pricing Officer (‘TPO’). The threshold limit of Rs. 5 crores is kept initially for returns filed for the assessment year 2002-03 which is reviewed from time-to-time. The limit has now been increased to Rs. 15 crores.

One such reference was challenged by the assessee in the case of Aztec Software & Technology Services Ltd. v. Asstt. CIT [2007] 109 ITD 141 (Bang.) (SB) and the Tribunal in a proactive manner to avoid future disputes set up a special bench of five members to look into such subject and other legal aspects in the transfer-pricing prodigy. The following crossfire happened since the time the reference came under challenge.

DECISION OF COMMISSIONER (APPEALS)

2. The Commissioner (Appeals) laid down various legal propositions in this case :

u That the invocation of the provisions under Chapter X of the Act pre-supposes existence of avoidance of tax by way of transfer price mechanism;

u That the heading of Chapter X of the Act - ‘Special Provisions relating to Avoidance of Tax’ assumed importance;

u That the Budget Speech of the Finance Minister for the year 2001 showed serious concern to the subject of allocation of profits in intra-group international transactions;

u That the Notes on Clauses to the Finance Act, 2001 are self-explaining;

u That the Memorandum of Finance Bill, 2001 insists that Chapter X was introduced as a measure to curb tax avoidance;

u That Circular No. 12 dated August 23, 2001 and Circular No. 14 of 2001 also speak of such special measures;

u That the use of the concept of the ‘Mischief Rule’ or ‘Heydon’s Rule’ in interpreting the provisions contained in Chapter X of the Act is worth-noticing;

u That the Assessing Officer has to first form an opinion or record his satisfaction on the basis of material or information or document in his possession that there is a valid reason for avoiding the tax burden in India;

u That the reference to the provisions of old section 52 of the Act is essential to be noted so that revenue must prove the avoidance of tax before invoking the provisions of Chapter X of the Act;

u That before embarking on any determination of ALP either by himself or by reference, the Assessing Officer has to pass through the process as prescribed under clauses (a) to (d) of sub-section (3) of section 92C of the Act, i.e., drawing a prima facie satisfaction that the international transaction price is not guided by arm’s length principle or clauses (a) to (d) of section 92C are satisfied;

u That there must be rational connection between the material/information, on one hand, and formation of the opinion, on the other hand. Consequently, the invoking of the provisions of Chapter X, in the absence of such satisfaction/formation of the opinion would be without jurisdiction and bad-in-law;

u That only once provisions of sub-section 3 of section 92C are complied with, the Assessing Officer can consider whether it is necessary or expedient to refer the determination of ALP to the TPO or in other words, section 99CA(1) itself does not have an independent existence and derives its strength and relevance from section 92C;

